"use strict";(self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara=self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara||[]).push([["74485"],{52387(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"FIN-051","slug":"simple-interest","term":"Simple Interest","also_known_as":[],"difficulty":"Beginner","definition":"Simple interest is interest calculated only on the original amount borrowed or saved, not on any interest already earned or charged.","explanation":["With simple interest, the interest each period is always the same, because it is always based on the original principal. It does not grow over time.","Suppose you save 5,000 at a simple interest rate of 4% a year. Each year you earn 200, which is 4% of 5,000. After three years you have earned 600 in interest, and your balance is 5,600. The fourth year earns another 200, no more.","This makes simple interest easy to calculate: multiply the principal by the annual rate and by the number of years. Here, 5,000 \xd7 0.04 \xd7 3 = 600.","Simple interest is common for short-term loans, some car loans and certain bonds that pay a fixed amount of interest each year. Most savings accounts, credit cards and long-term investments, however, use compound interest, where interest is added to the balance and then earns interest itself. Over long periods, the difference between the two can be very large."],"note":"","total":1500,"prev":{"slug":"signaling-theory","term":"Signaling Theory"},"next":{"slug":"sinking-fund","term":"Sinking Fund"}}')}}]);